# Games, each played far enough in to show a board, a hand or a room
# rather than a title screen.  One stanza per program; every stanza makes
# whatever it needs for itself.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adventure

shot    advcom
size    30 80
cap     The ADVSYS compiler: it turns an adventure written in ADVSYS source
cap     into the world file the interpreter plays, naming every actor,
cap     location and thing as it goes and counting the words, objects and
cap     code at the end.  The sample adventure is in GAMES/ADVSYS.  advcom
cap     opens its `@objects.adi' include by bare name in the data directory
cap     and keeps a filename in twenty characters, so both files are copied
cap     to a working directory and it is run there, under a shell whose
cap     `chd' moves the data directory.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/osample.adv
wait    4
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/objects.adi
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advcom
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     sh -c "chd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; advcom osample"
wait    45

shot    advint
size    24 80
cap     The ADVSYS interpreter, playing the world advcom has just built from
cap     the sample source: you start in the livingroom, `n' takes you to the
cap     hallway and `e' into the storage room, where there is a rusty key.
cap     Type at the `:' prompt.  Like the compiler it opens its world file by
cap     bare name, so it is run in the directory holding it.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/osample.adv
wait    4
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/objects.adi
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advcom
wait    3
run     sh -c "chd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; advcom osample" > /nil
wait    45
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advint
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     sh -c "chd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; advint osample"
wait    12
send    look\r
wait    5
send    n\r
wait    5
send    e\r
wait    6

shot    advent
cap     Colossal Cave, the first adventure of them all.  It loads its twelve
cap     sections of text, offers instructions, and drops you on the road
cap     outside the brick building; `in' takes you to the well house, where
cap     the lamp and the keys are, and `xyzzy' is the magic word.  Its world
cap     file lives in GAMES/adv.
run     advent
wait    8
send    no\r
wait    5
send    in\r
wait    5
send    take lamp\r
wait    5
send    take keys\r
wait    5
send    xyzzy\r
wait    6

shot    infocom.tcap
cap     An interpreter for Infocom's Z-machine, playing a story file: a
cap     status line across the top with the room name and the score, the
cap     text below it, and a `>' prompt for commands like `look' and
cap     `inventory'.  Three Inform demonstration stories ship in
cap     GAMES/INFORM -- dejavu, hellow and shell.  This build reads the
cap     terminal description from termcap, and is the one to use at a
cap     terminal.
run     infocom.tcap /dd/GAMES/INFORM/dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    look\r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    4
send    inventory\r
wait    6

shot    infocom
cap     The same Z-machine interpreter without the termcap display code.  It
cap     plays the story, but it writes the cursor codes for its status line
cap     as literal text, which is the row of brackets down the left.  For a
cap     readable screen use infocom.tcap; RETURN clears each [MORE].
run     infocom /dd/GAMES/INFORM/dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    5
send    look\r
wait    6

shot    paranoia
cap     The Paranoia text adventure.  It rolls you a Troubleshooter --
cap     attributes, secret society, mutant power, equipment -- and then
cap     wakes you on the floor of a briefing room.  RETURN clears each
cap     `More' and goes on; you get six clones.
run     paranoia
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    5

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- arcade

shot    greed
cap     Greed: the screen is a field of digits and `@' is you.  Move with
cap     the vi keys and you eat that many digits in that direction, scoring
cap     them and leaving blanks behind; the game ends when no move is left.
cap     The percentage beside the score is how much of the field you have
cap     cleared.
run     greed
wait    8
keys    jjkl
wait    5
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    lander
cap     A lunar lander flown over a scrolling landscape.  Space starts a
cap     descent, a digit sets the engine power, `x' or `k' fires the main
cap     thruster and `z'/`j' and `c'/`l' the side retros; the numbers on the
cap     pads are what each is worth.  The top line is altitude, position and
cap     the two rates of drift, the bottom line thrust and fuel.  Its score
cap     table is GAMES/lander.hs.
run     lander
wait    8
send    \s
wait    4
send    5
wait    3
send    x
wait    4
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    pacman
cap     Pac-Man written for G-Windows, the graphics system this disk does
cap     not carry.  At a terminal it draws nothing and returns to the shell
cap     at once, which is the whole of what it does here; its maze and score
cap     file are in GAMES/PACMAN.
run     pacman
wait    8

shot    robots
cap     Robots: you are the `I', the robots are the `#', and they walk
cap     straight at you every time you move -- steer them into each other
cap     and they become junk heaps.  Run it with `-m' so they take one step
cap     per move of yours; the keys are the numeric keypad 1-9 with 5 to
cap     stand still, `t' to teleport and `s' for a last stand.
run     robots -m
wait    8
keys    8
wait    3
keys    8
wait    3
keys    9
wait    4
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    snake
cap     Snake: you are the `I', the money is the `$' and the snake is the
cap     line of `s' with `S' for its head.  Move with h, j, k and l and
cap     reach the money before the snake reaches you; `x' quits.  Some of
cap     its cursor moves arrive as text, so the board picks up stray
cap     characters as you play.
run     snake
wait    10
snap
send    x
wait    3

shot    sokoban
cap     Sokoban: push every packet, the `$', onto a storage square, the
cap     `.', without trapping one in a corner.  `@' is you.  The status line
cap     counts the level, the packets, the ones stored and your moves and
cap     pushes.  Its fifty levels, its help text and its saved games are in
cap     GAMES/SOKOBAN, and it asks the system for your user name, so run it
cap     from a login.
run     sokoban
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    tet
cap     Tetris, with the well on the left and the menu on the right: `p'
cap     plays, s or j moves the falling piece left, f or l right, d or k
cap     turns it, space drops it, ESC pauses and `q' leaves to the
cap     high-score table it keeps in GAMES/tet.hs.  It wants a real
cap     terminal; from a pipe it draws the board and reads nothing.
run     tet
wait    8
keys    p
wait    5
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    4
snap
send    q
wait    3

shot    wanderer
cap     Wanderer, a Boulderdash: `<|>' is you, the dots are earth you can
cap     dig through and the diamonds are what you are after -- forty-five on
cap     the first screen, with a thousand moves to get them.  Its thirty
cap     screens are in GAMES/WAND.
run     wanderer
wait    10
snap
send    q
wait    3

# -------------------------------------------------------------- board & card

shot    back
cap     Backgammon drawn as a full board, points numbered 1 to 24, with the
cap     dice cup and the doubling status on the right.  Single letters are
cap     the commands: `R' rolls, `D' doubles, `H' is the help, `N' starts a
cap     new game and `Q' quits.
run     back
wait    20
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    blackjack
cap     A Las Vegas blackjack in BASIC09 (M. Theys).  Like the other BASIC09
cap     programs it runs through Microware's `runb' -- `load /h1/CMDS/runb'
cap     then `runb blackjack' -- from your own OS-9.  It sits in CMDS/BROKEN,
cap     off the login path; for a blackjack that runs here, see `blackjak'.
run     /dd/CMDS/BROKEN/blackjack
wait    8

shot    blackjak
cap     Las Vegas blackjack, with a hand dealt and a bet on the table: it
cap     starts you with 21 chips, asks what you want to bet, deals two cards
cap     against the dealer's one, and then asks whether to hit or stick.
cap     It wants the word `no', not `N'.  One of four games written in
cap     SNOBOL4; their data is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
run     blackjak
wait    6
send    no\r
wait    5
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    chess
cap     Chess against the machine on a shaded board, one move in: it asks
cap     which colour you want, your name and whether the game is long or
cap     short, then takes a move as two squares -- `e2' for the piece and
cap     `e4' for where it goes, two keystrokes each with no RETURN.  Its
cap     reply is on the line under the board.
run     chess
wait    8
keys    w
wait    4
send    Tester\r
wait    4
keys    s
wait    6
keys    e2
wait    5
keys    e4
wait    25
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    crib
cap     Cribbage: your hand fanned out on the left, the two pegging tracks
cap     on the right, and the cut that decides whose crib it is.  Discard by
cap     naming a card -- `7H' for the seven of hearts.  It needs TERM set,
cap     so run it from a login session.  Its instructions are in
cap     GAMES/CRIB.
run     crib
wait    8
send    n\r
wait    4
send    S\r
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    cribbage
cap     A second cribbage, drawn the same way but a different program: the
cap     board on the right, the dialogue under it, and the same cut for the
cap     crib.  It offers you the rules first.  Like `crib' it needs TERM.
run     cribbage
wait    8
send    n\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    gnuan
size    30 80
cap     GNU Chess's analyser: give it a file of moves and a search depth and
cap     it annotates the game move by move, scoring each and naming the best
cap     line it found.  Moves it recognises from the opening book are marked
cap     `book'.  At the end of the file it prints the position reached and
cap     stops with `Bad move', which is how it reads end of input.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil; echo "e2e4 e7e5 g1f3 b8c6 f1b5 a7a6" > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/moves
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuan
wait    8
send    /dd/tmp/GAMESA/moves\r
wait    5
send    2\r
wait    4
send    1\r
wait    30

shot    gnuchess
size    24 80
cap     GNU Chess after 1. e4, with a clock for each side and the search
cap     depth on the right.  It asks for a time control first -- `1' is one
cap     move a minute -- and then takes moves as `e2e4'.  It reads the
cap     terminal description out of TERMCAP as the description itself rather
cap     than as a filename, so the entry is put in the variable first.
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     /dd/CMDS/GAMES/gnuchess
wait    12
send    1\r
wait    10
send    e2e4\r
wait    22
kill

shot    gnuchessc
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 built for a curses display.  Its display files are
cap     compiled to a path this disk does not carry, so it draws no board;
cap     it still plays, taking a move as `e2e4' and printing its own reply
cap     on the next line.  For a board, see `gnuchess' or `nchess'.
run     gnuchessc
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    gnuchessn
cap     A GNU Chess display from 1989, which draws the squares as blocks of
cap     hashes so that the light and dark ones can be told apart on a
cap     terminal with no highlighting.  Moves go in as `e2e4'.  It reads
cap     TERMCAP as the terminal description itself, so the entry is put in
cap     the variable first.
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuchessn
wait    14
send    e2e4\r
wait    15
kill

shot    gnuchessr
cap     GNU Chess with the plainest display of all -- pieces as letters,
cap     capitals for one side and lower case for the other, nothing that
cap     needs a terminal to draw.  It asks for the time control as
cap     `#moves #minutes' and then takes moves as `e2e4'.
run     gnuchessr
wait    10
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    mille
cap     Mille Bornes, the French road-race card game: your hand on the left
cap     numbered 1 to 6, the deck and the discard beside it, the scoring
cap     table on the right and the commands under it -- `p' picks, `u #'
cap     plays a card, `d #' discards, `s' saves the game and `q' quits.
run     mille
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    nchess
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with its plain display, thinking: the board in
cap     letters, then a line for each depth it searched showing the score,
cap     the time, the nodes and the line it likes, and the totals at the
cap     foot.  It asks for a time control as `#moves #minutes' first.
run     nchess
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    poker
cap     Cold-hand poker: it asks what you want to bet, deals, and lets you
cap     draw.  It wants the word `no', not `N', and says so in its own
cap     voice if you try.  One of four games written in SNOBOL4; their data
cap     is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
run     poker
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    50\r
wait    6
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    queens
cap     The eight-queens problem for any board: it reads the size as a
cap     number on standard input and draws every arrangement it finds with
cap     no two queens attacking.  Six here.
run     echo 6 | queens | head -n 21
wait    8

shot    tttt
cap     Tic-tac-toe on a four-by-four board, two moves in.  Name a square by
cap     its column letter and row digit -- `b1' -- and the machine answers.
run     tttt
wait    4
send    b1\r
wait    3
send    c2\r
wait    5
snap
send    q
wait    2

# --------------------------------------------------------- chess utilities

shot    bincheckr
cap     Checks a GNU Chess opening book for how well it is packed: entry
cap     size, book size, pocket and count.  Given the 145 KB book that ships
cap     in GAMES it reports the entry size and then zeroes for the rest, and
cap     is aborted as it closes the file.
run     bincheckr /dd/GAMES/gnuchess.book
wait    10

shot    checkgame
size    30 80
cap     Reads a saved GNU Chess game -- the `chess.lst' that gnuchess writes
cap     when you type `list' -- replays it looking for illegal moves and
cap     prints the board it finishes on.  A game is made here first by
cap     playing one move against gnuchess and listing it.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil; rm -f /dd/tmp/GAMESA/chess.lst
wait    3
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    3
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/gnuchess
wait    4
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; gnuchess" > /nil 2>/nil
wait    12
send    1\r
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    20
send    list\r
wait    8
send    quit\r
wait    6
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat /dd/tmp/GAMESA/chess.lst
wait    5
run     checkgame /dd/tmp/GAMESA/chess.lst
wait    10

shot    game
size    24 80
cap     Meant to draw a saved GNU Chess game board by board as PostScript,
cap     one page a move, for printing with the ChessFont file its
cap     documentation names.  Given a game it writes the first line of the
cap     page setup and stops there, so there is nothing to print.
cap     DOC/game has the manual page.
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil; echo "e2e4      0      0      0     8" > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/g.lst
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     game /dd/tmp/GAMESA/g.lst 1 2
wait    12

shot    postprint
cap     Meant to print the positions in GNU Chess's saved hash file as
cap     PostScript, a board to a page with the best move and the search
cap     depth.  Given GNU Chess's opening book in place of a saved hash it
cap     writes the first line of the page setup and stops.  DOC/postprint
cap     has the manual page.
run     postprint /dd/GAMES/gnuchess.book
wait    12

# ------------------------------------------------------------ dungeon crawl

shot    hack
cap     Hack, the dungeon crawl NetHack grew out of: a room drawn in line
cap     characters, `@' for you, a letter for each monster -- `d' is a dog
cap     -- and your hit points, armour class, strength and experience along
cap     the foot.  Start it by its full path, because it looks itself up by
cap     the name it was called with after moving into its playground, which
cap     is GAMES/HACK/PLAYGROUND.
run     /dd/CMDS/GAMES/hack
wait    10
send    n\r
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    larn
cap     Larn: the dungeon is drawn as you find it, letters for what is in
cap     it, and the two lines at the foot are your spells, armour class,
cap     level and the six attributes.  RETURN gets past the opening text.
cap     Its saved games and score file are in GAMES/LARN/PLAYGROUND.
run     larn
wait    12
send    \r
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    ularn
cap     ULarn, the larn variant: it asks you to pick a character class --
cap     ogre, wizard, klingon, elf, rogue, geek, dwarf or rambo -- and then
cap     drops you in the dungeon with the same two status lines.  Its
cap     fortunes, help, maps and scores are in GAMES/ULARN.
run     ularn
wait    10
keys    b
wait    5
send    \r
wait    5
snap
send    \003
wait    3

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ puzzles

shot    maze
cap     A maze generator small enough to have won an obfuscated-C contest.
cap     It reads the number of rows on standard input and draws a maze that
cap     wide across the terminal.
run     echo 11 | maze
wait    8

shot    mines
cap     Minesweeper on a sixteen-by-sixteen board with forty mines.  Name a
cap     square by its row letter and then its column letter; answer the
cap     `Mark?' prompt with Y to flag it instead of opening it.  Opening a
cap     square clears everything around it that is safe and numbers the
cap     edges with how many mines they touch.  `q' quits.
run     mines
wait    8
send    H\r
wait    3
send    H\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    5
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    puz15
cap     The fifteen puzzle, shuffled: slide the tiles into the gap until
cap     they read 1 to 15.  It counts your moves and your time.  Give it a
cap     size to play on a different board -- `puz15 5x5'.
run     puz15
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    puzzle15
cap     The same fifteen puzzle under its longer name, in CMDS rather than
cap     CMDS/GAMES.  Slide the tiles into the gap; the move count and clock
cap     are along the foot.  DOC/puzzle15 has its notes.
run     puzzle15
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

# ----------------------------------------------------------- word & guessing

shot    animal
cap     The guess-the-animal game, which learns: it asks yes-or-no questions
cap     down a tree of things it already knows and makes a final guess, and
cap     when it is wrong it asks you what you were thinking of and what
cap     question would have told the two apart, then writes the answer back
cap     into its data file.  A file to start from is in DOC/animal.
run     animal /dd/DOC/animal/example
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    3
send    y\r
wait    4
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    bog
cap     Boggle: sixteen lettered dice in a four-by-four tray and three
cap     minutes on the clock to type every word you can trace through
cap     adjoining letters.  `?' shows the rules.  Its word list, index and
cap     help text are in GAMES/BOG.
run     bog
wait    8
send    \s
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    hang
cap     Hangman: the word as blanks, the letters still unused along the top
cap     and the gallows filling in as you get them wrong.  Type a letter to
cap     guess it.  Its word list is GAMES/dict.
run     hang
wait    6
keys    aeiort
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

# -------------------------------------------------------------- other games

shot    fuddle
cap     fuddle, a chess variant, one move in.
run     fuddle
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    hotel
cap     hotel, a two-player board game played by coordinates.  It asks how
cap     many are playing and then draws the board.
run     hotel
wait    5
send    N\r
wait    4
send    2\r
wait    5
send    Alice\r
wait    4
send    Bob\r
wait    8

shot    stone
cap     The stones game: take stones from the pile, do not take the last
cap     one.  Twenty-one stones, the parity chosen, and the first move
cap     made.  It answers a wrong answer in its own voice.
run     stone
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    21\r
wait    6
send    1\r
wait    6
send    3\r
wait    8

shot    draw
for     draw
cap     draw, Nils McCarthy's character-graphics drawing program: it rules a
cap     canvas across the terminal and you move a cursor over it laying down
cap     characters.  This is what `?' shows -- vi's hjkl to move, yubn for
cap     the diagonals, `p' to lift and drop the pen, backslash to choose the
cap     character it draws with, and a shell escape.  The keys can be
cap     rebound through a DRAW environment variable.
run     draw
wait    8
keys    ?
wait    6
kill

shot    lorenz3d
for     lorenz3d
cap     The Lorenz attractor in three dimensions, asked for parameter by
cap     parameter: the time step, the z magnification and offset, then
cap     Lorenz's own a, b and c -- 10, 28 and 8/3 here, the values that
cap     make the butterfly.  What it draws with is TEKTRONIX vector codes,
cap     so on a vt100 the picture arrives as characters; the dialogue is
cap     the part worth capturing.
run     lorenz3d
wait    4
send    0.01\r
wait    2
send    20\r
wait    2
send    0\r
wait    2
send    10\r
wait    2
send    28\r
wait    2
send    2.667\r
wait    2
kill


# The rest of "Other games", one stanza each.

shot    tt
cap     Tetris for terminals, a few pieces in: , and / move a piece, .
cap     rotates it, space drops it, s pauses and q quits.
run     tt
wait    4
keys    ,,.
wait    2
send    \s
wait    3
keys    //
wait    2
send    \s
wait    3
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    zot
cap     Prints a line of text in one of fourteen decorative styles; -d
cap     shows them all in turn.  The styles are terminal attributes --
cap     bold, underline, blink -- so on this page they all look alike.
run     zot -d "OS-9 freeware"
wait    8

shot    convert
cap     Starts the WORLD text adventure: its opening screen, waiting for
cap     a key at MORE.
run     convert
wait    5
send    \r
wait    6
snap
send    \r
wait    4
snap
send    quit\r
wait    3

shot    vtxtcn
cap     Builds WORLD's text tables from the .dat files in the current
cap     directory, writing .inc files and printing nothing.
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/GAMES/WORLD; vtxtcn" < /nil; echo "status $?"
wait    12
run     ls /dd/GAMES/WORLD
wait    4

shot    mkindex
cap     Builds the index bog reads its dictionary through, in the
cap     directory holding the dictionary; it reports two counts.
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/GAMES/BOG; mkindex" < /nil; echo "status $?"
wait    12

shot    mkdict
cap     Builds bog's dictionary from a word list.  On the list that ships
cap     it takes a bus error and stops.
run     ksh -c "cd /dd/GAMES/BOG; mkdict" < /nil; echo "status $?"
wait    12

shot    ask
cap     Reads the next message from the pipe wisecrack writes to and
cap     prints it, one a call; with wisecrack running behind it the
cap     messages come out one at a time.
run     wisecrack &
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     ask
wait    5
run     ask
wait    5

shot    shuffle
cap     A switch puzzle: a row of numbered switches where flipping one
cap     flips its neighbours, a level and a move counter.  q quits.
run     shuffle
wait    6
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    cyberwar
cap     A game written for G-Windows, the graphics system this disk does
cap     not carry: at a terminal it exits without a word.
run     cyberwar; echo "status $?"
wait    6

shot    puzzle
cap     A sliding-tile puzzle for G-Windows: at a terminal it gets one
cap     line of the tile frame out and stops.  puzzle15 is the one to play.
run     puzzle
wait    6

shot    scriptmaster
cap     A scripting tool for G-Windows: at a terminal it exits without a
cap     word.
run     scriptmaster; echo "status $?"
wait    5

shot    colortest
cap     A colour chart for G-Windows: it looks for the /win device and
cap     says it cannot.
run     colortest
wait    6

shot    dclock
cap     A digital clock for G-Windows, and it says so.
run     dclock; echo "status $?"
wait    5
